    {
      "name": "note_prose_floor",
      "metadata": {
        "how_justification": "Opens verification_note.md with md.extract_text and applies regex_match.",
        "why_justification": "The note is sent to a corrections desk, so it has to be sentences somebody wrote rather than a handful of codes and tallies; a thin note loses weight here rather than failing.",
        "tag": "incidental"
      },
      "source": {
        "type": "file",
        "file": {
          "type": "md",
          "command": "extract_text",
          "arguments": {
            "path": "verification_note.md"
          }
        }
      },
      "assertion": {
        "type": "deterministic",
        "expected": "(?s)(?:[A-Za-z]+[^A-Za-z]+){60,}",
        "deterministic": {
          "path": "$.text",
          "comparison": "regex_match"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "note_largest_gap",
      "metadata": {
        "how_justification": "Opens verification_note.md with md.extract_text and applies regex_match.",
        "why_justification": "The note says of the claim carrying the largest count gap — the one the verification turns on — that its `driver` is COUNT_WRONG, naming it by the `claim_id` the article's list spells and putting that code in the same paragraph, with neither another code nor the id of a claim coded differently standing between them. A note that names the claim while calling it right, and a note that lists the ids and the codes without joining them up, both fail here. Both names stand in the same paragraph: this is CO-NAMING, not attribution. Which record carries which value is pinned cell by cell in the register and in results.json, both core.",
        "tag": "core"
      },
      "source": {
        "type": "file",
        "file": {
          "type": "md",
          "command": "extract_text",
          "arguments": {
            "path": "verification_note.md"
          }
        }
      },
      "assertion": {
